It's a spacious one-bedroom, it's completely modern, has beautiful views, modern appliances, and central air. And rent is only a little higher than the place she started out in. Now, she's exceptionally lucky. Less than 10% of those who were in the project site qualify for housing in the new project, and even fewer of them will make it on the waiting list in time. So, uh, what just happened?

81
00:38:19,370 --> 00:38:48,680
What you do during slum clearance is you start to really shuffle people around a bunch within the project area. Once you've harassed enough families with the means to move out, you can start shoving people from the most vacant buildings to ones with fewer vacancies, then demolish those newly empty buildings. You can also hit those families with a rent hike simultaneously, giving them more incentive to leave. During slum clearances for New York City public housing,

82
00:38:49,300 --> 00:39:16,820
orchestrated by Robert Moses, who I can't believe we're only talking about now for the first time on this channel. Families were frequently shuffled from building to building half a dozen times or more in a single year, with a rent hike each time. Since these buildings were all slated for demolition, of course, maintenance was pretty minimal. The heating failed, the water was shut off, intentionally or unintentionally.

83
00:39:17,080 --> 00:39:44,580
Windows broke and were never fixed, and generally life was made as miserable as possible for those who were unwilling or unable to move out of the project area. In the meantime, you start construction on the cleared area of the project while demolitions are still ongoing in other areas, frequently adjacent to still-occupied buildings, to cause maximum noise and dust and generally inconvenience the remaining tenants to encourage them to leave.

84
00:39:46,650 --> 00:40:15,580
token efforts at providing relocation assistance were provided, like paying for moving expenses and showing some apartments to soon-to-be-displaced tenants. These apartments were almost universally more expensive than the slums being cleared, and usually the relocation agency had many, many, many more tenants to relocate than apartments available. So, in short, if you lived in a slum being cleared, you got screwed.

85
00:40:19,260 --> 00:40:41,090
Now that you've learned how these projects are built, let's look at how they're maintained. Let's look at Pruitt & Igoe. So, Pruitt & Igoe was designed as a modern public housing project to replace a neighborhood which was actually a slum, not a fake slum, to hand out to developers in St. Louis.

86
00:40:41,840 --> 00:41:11,530
It was initially designed to be segregated, but segregation was made illegal while it was under construction, so that idea got tossed out. It comprised 33 11-story buildings, totaling 2,870 apartments. By the way, you can get this story in much more detail from the Pruitt-Igoe myth, which is currently available in full on YouTube, so I'll put the link to that in the description.

87
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:40,540
Now, these towers, when they were first built, were modern, clean, spacious, air-conditioned, and so on. When the tenants moved in, everyone was mostly happy with their apartments, and, you know, you had a pretty harmonious community for a while. But some financial issues started to creep into the project pretty early on. So, this thing called the Federal Housing Administration...

88
00:41:40,810 --> 00:42:07,480
was offering extremely low-cost financing on suburban homes across the country. This essentially meant that owning a home in the suburbs was almost universally cheaper than renting in the city. So, racial covenants had been made illegal in 1948. That's a stipulation that you can't sell a plot of land to people of a certain race. So...

89
00:42:07,800 --> 00:42:34,710
There was no legal way to discriminate by race in Federal Housing Administration mortgages. But that never stopped a mortgage broker before. You could throw out any application for any reason, so long as you didn't mention race was the reason. So, in St. Louis proper, whites were leaving the city to take advantage of low-cost mortgages in the suburbs.

90
00:42:35,620 --> 00:43:06,840
A lot of blacks were moving in from the south, but not at the rate to replace the whites who were leaving. Meanwhile, a major housing project had just been completed, which was designed to serve a growing city, which was now shrinking, and the project was not financially stable unless it was fully occupied. Do you see a problem here? So, while Pruitt-Igoe was fully occupied when it opened, it started to empty out fairly quickly.

91
00:43:08,360 --> 00:43:31,350
And there are a couple reasons for this beyond simple racism. We have to go back to the United States welfare system's bizarre tendency to attempt to control and coerce folks' behaviors. Right, so a well-meaning policy, which we still sort of have today, is that public housing is reserved for the lowest earning folks with the least ability to earn income, which...

92
00:43:31,870 --> 00:43:57,760
In the Pruitt-Igoe era meant black single mothers, since motherhood was still considered a full-time job and the authorities assumed you'd be unable to support yourself. So if you wanted out of the slums and into the new, modern, clean public housing system, you either had to be a single mother or you had to fake it. And a lot of folks desperate to leave the slums faked it.

93
00:43:59,790 --> 00:44:23,950
So, this incentive structure led to perverse outcomes. Fathers would deliberately move out of state so that their children and wives would be eligible for public housing. Families had to keep up a farce that the father had left for cigarettes and never come back or something like that, when in fact he was living just across the river in some East St. Louis hell shack, and he made sure to visit most days.

94
00:44:25,710 --> 00:44:55,330
Now, this situation was well known to the housing authority who would actually send in inspectors to folks' units to make damn well sure there wasn't a happy and healthy nuclear family living in the unit. If they found a father in what was supposed to be a single mother's unit, then everyone got the boot. Fathers had to hide in closets or cupboards whenever the inspector came around. And this is in addition to the fact that you'd be kicked out of your apartment if you made too much money.

95
00:44:55,950 --> 00:45:22,550
or for a whole host of trivial other things. Sometimes I think that conservatives are onto something when they talk about American welfare as being a method of social control, but it damn well does not manifest in the ways they think it does. Your normal conservative complaints about, you know, deadbeat black fathers ring kind of hollow when, you know, the benefits are entirely inaccessible to intact families.

