00:47:19,660 --> 00:47:49,350
is about the only way to make their displeasure with the management of the complex known. But that's obviously not to say that was the only resistance. Far from it. In 1968, the residents of Pruitt-Igoe, led by Bertha Gilkey, went on a nine-month rent strike to protest the conditions of the development. An organized contingent of residents collected the rent each month and held it from the housing authority.

101
00:47:49,760 --> 00:48:19,960
until they received their demands, including a comprehensive plan to fix Pruitt-Igoe. By the end of the strike, they had won a commitment from the housing authority to better maintain the buildings and find a long-term solution. But the long-term solution they came up with did not include Pruitt-Igoe. The first buildings were demolished in 1972. The less than 20-year-old buildings were imploded on national television.

102
00:48:20,500 --> 00:48:50,280
And a lot of people say with them went the nation's confidence in the ability of government to solve their problems. By 1976, the entire development was gone, and the community scattered throughout St. Louis and beyond. So, the residual memory of Pruitt-Igoe tends to be what we think of when we think of public housing in the United States. But is it really the case that all public housing developments descended into mismanagement and madness?

