104
00:49:20,070 --> 00:49:36,400
You know, not fantastic, but it wasn't a failure. But the real thing I draw attention to here is that not all public housing projects are high-rises, and those non-high-rise projects tend to be more successful. Why is this?

105
00:49:37,930 --> 00:50:08,580
A lot of people point to the architecture, you know, they'll say the proportions of a tower are inhuman, or somehow an apartment on the 10th story of an 11th story building turns you into a monster, while the second floor apartment of identical size on a two-story building lets you remain an upstanding member of the community. But the real answer is a little more material than that. Small buildings are easier and cheaper to maintain. That's it, that's the whole answer.

106
00:50:09,420 --> 00:50:36,950
Economies of scale don't work too well for really big apartment buildings. The sweet spot is usually around four to five stories. If I'm running a public housing development with two to three story buildings, I don't need a bunch of elevators. I might need one at most for moving in and moving out and for Americans with Disabilities Act compliance, which is important. I don't need a complicated high pressure fire protection system.

107
00:50:37,870 --> 00:51:05,960
I don't need water pumps or a water tower. I don't need an on-site incinerator or commercial-scale trash collection. I don't need steel or concrete construction. I can get away with wood or load-bearing brick. I'll never have to worry about minor facade issues costing tens of thousands of dollars to fix, since I can just get a guy up there on a man lift in one day instead of contracting a specialized company to set up a swing stage 150 feet in the air.

108
00:51:06,670 --> 00:51:34,100
The two-story building is just easier and cheaper to maintain. And if you're charging the same amount in rent that you do for the towers, you have more than enough money to maintain these smaller buildings. And chances are, you are charging the same amount as you would for the towers. And, you know, maybe you're making up some in subsidy or something. And the other part of this is the concept of eyes on the street.

109
00:51:35,270 --> 00:51:57,890
So in your big tower block, you don't have a view into the common areas, right? You don't see into the hallway until you open your door. You don't necessarily know what's going on in the hallway except for like some muffled sounds. You definitely don't know what's going on in the stairwell, despite the fact you're going to have to use it if the elevator isn't working, which it often isn't.

110
00:51:58,560 --> 00:52:23,450
If the elevator is working, you don't know who's going to be on there or what's going to be in there, which is bad if you're going to be stuck in there for a while with another person you don't necessarily trust. In your two-story building, you have a direct view onto the street through your front window. You know what's going on, and folks out there on the street know they might be being watched. So there's a little less chance for shenanigans.

